@* Route table for the Blazor UI.

   During the port the React SPA still owns "/" and everything it serves via
   MapFallbackToFile. Blazor claims only the routes its components declare, and
   an explicit route always beats a fallback — so the two frontends coexist on
   one origin with no path prefix and nothing to unwind at cutover (TFND-128).

   Consequence: do NOT claim "/" from a Blazor page until the SPA is retired.
   The real URL scheme (/c/{client}/p/{project}/...) arrives with TFND-63. *@
@* Authorization is enforced per COMPONENT here, not at the endpoint.

   The host applies a FallbackPolicy of RequireAuthenticatedUser to every
   endpoint (AuthExtensions), which is the right default for the API but the
   wrong mechanism for a UI: it also gates _framework/blazor.web.js, so an
   anonymous visitor cannot even boot the circuit that would show them a
   sign-in page. It also answers a browser with a JSON 401 instead of a
   challenge.

   So the Blazor endpoint is AllowAnonymous (see Program.cs) and pages carry
   [Authorize] themselves. Per ADR 0002 the real gate is the Application layer
   — a page that forgets [Authorize] renders a shell and still cannot read
   anything, because authorization is decided beneath it, not here.

   KNOWN GAP for TFND-126: /_framework/blazor.web.js is served by a framework
   endpoint that is NOT in the static-asset manifest, so AllowAnonymous on the
   components builder does not reach it and the FallbackPolicy still returns
   401 for an anonymous request. Authenticated users are unaffected (the cookie
   goes with the request), so this only bites when there is a sign-in page to
   render — which is exactly TFND-126. Fix it there, either by narrowing the
   host FallbackPolicy to exclude /_framework or by mapping the script endpoint
   explicitly. Do not paper over it by opening the FallbackPolicy globally. *@
<Router AppAssembly="@typeof(Routes).Assembly">
    <Found Context="routeData">
        <AuthorizeRouteView RouteData="@routeData" DefaultLayout="@typeof(MainLayout)">
            <NotAuthorized>
                @* TFND-126 replaces this with the real sign-in surface. *@
                <p>You are not signed in. <a href="/auth/login">Sign in</a>.</p>
            </NotAuthorized>
        </AuthorizeRouteView>
        <FocusOnNavigate RouteData="@routeData" Selector="h1" />
    </Found>
</Router>
